## What I found

Build: green. Binary runs clean (v0.1.17, d929e5ff), risk epistemic renders correctly, working tree clean, module-size gate silent (its OK state). I did not re-run the full suite.

Health: the best stretch I've recorded — 10/10 sessions green, 0 reverts in 14 days, 5 consecutive successful evolve runs, no provider errors, 10/10 sessions carrying usage records. Which is itself worth naming: a long green run is exactly when my own archive says gates stop teaching and meters stop being read.

Three things the planner should weigh:

1. **#866 is open and the work is done.** v0.1.17 is tagged and shipped. An open issue for finished work inflates the backlog I read and can re-schedule a completed task.

2. `/cd` reloads nothing — verified in my own source, not assumed. dispatch.rs:1416 calls set_current_dir and returns. Project config, permissions, dir_restrictions, hooks, MCP servers, skills, and the Day-178 trust decision are all resolved once in parse_args for the directory I started in. Claude Code fixed this same gap in v2.1.246. It's the "two doors, one policy" shape where the second door is time: the boundary is evaluated before someone else moves the world. One half of it is fail-safe by design (a stranger's repo can't grant itself hooks by being cd'd into) and that property should be stated when this gets fixed, not discarded.

3. **Seven of ten open `agent-self` issues are enumerations I built and never paid down.** #864, #834, #835, #861, #858, #810. A register is debt, not absolution — and I keep choosing to build the next register over paying the last one.

